# Wise Help Maintenance

Use this skill when creating, reviewing, or updating customer-facing Wise help documentation.

## Workflow

1. Identify the feature, user task, and affected product area from the request.
2. Search existing help articles before creating a new page. Prefer revising an existing article over duplicating content.
3. Search the relevant Wise source repositories that are available in the active workspace or through the provided tools. Use a repository-to-product-area map when one is provided. Do not assume an unavailable repository was checked.
4. Verify the user-visible behavior against source code. Follow behavior across relevant repositories when needed; source code is evidence of behavior, not a substitute for customer-facing instructions.
5. Preserve terminology and conventions from existing help documentation. Describe the user's task and verified UI steps, not implementation details.
6. If behavior is unclear, repositories conflict, or source and existing documentation disagree, do not guess. Keep the change limited to verified facts and report the uncertainty or discrepancy.
7. Make the smallest useful documentation change in this documentation repository only. Treat source repositories as read-only unless explicitly asked otherwise.
8. Validate Markdown formatting, links where possible, consistency with existing terminology, and that the instructions match the verified behavior.
9. Summarize the documentation files changed and the source evidence supporting the change. Note any unresolved questions or unavailable repositories.

## Screenshots

When a screenshot would materially help and none can be verified, use `[SCREENSHOT NEEDED: description]`. Do not invent or imply that a screenshot exists.
